[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Oracle tagging/direct-sum construction: To combine finitely many sparse sets S1,...,Sk into one sparse oracle S, tag each element x ∈ Si with a constant-size identifier for i to form disjoint copies; define S = ⋃_{i=1}^k {tag(i) ∘ x : x ∈ Si}. Sparsity per length follows by summing polynomial bounds with only constant-length shifts.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Sparsity preservation under constant-length padding: If |Si^{=m}| ≤ pi(m) for polynomials pi, then for S^{=n} formed by adding a constant overhead ci to lengths, |S^{=n}| ≤ Σ_i pi(n − ci), which is polynomial in n.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - P_bad-angel equals P: If the “angel” advice αn is computable in polynomial time A(n), then a poly-time decider can compute αn and then run M(x, αn). Conversely, any P language fits P_bad-angel by choosing trivial advice αn (e.g., ε) and an M that ignores it. Therefore P_bad-angel = P. Consequently, NP = P_bad-angel iff P = NP.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Encoding advice into a sparse oracle via padding: Given L ∈ P_angel with polynomial p(n) and angel string αn ∈ {0,1}^{p(n)}, construct a sparse set SL that contains, for each length n, at most p(n) strings of a designated length t(n) encoding the positions j where αn[j] = 1. Choose t(n) to be a strictly increasing polynomial that dominates p(n) (e.g., t(n) = (n+1)^r with r ≥ deg p), so that for length m = t(n), the count is ≤ p(n) ≤ m^c for some constant c (e.g., c = 1 if r ≥ deg p), ensuring sparsity. A poly-time oracle TM can reconstruct αn by querying all j ∈ [1..p(n)] and then simulate the original M(x, αn).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Pitfall Prediction:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Conceptual: 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - Accidentally using a delimiter symbol outside {0,1}; must encode tags and delimiters with bits only.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - Treating k as growing with input length; here k is fixed, so the tag overhead is constant for sparsity.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - Confusing “advice” with NP witnesses; P_angel’s αn is per length, not per input, and can be non-computable; P_bad-angel’s αn must be poly-time computable.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Calculation:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - Failing to bound |S^{=n}| polynomially when aggregating multiple sets; must account for constant-length overhead correctly.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - Choosing t(n) too small so that p(n) is not bounded by a polynomial in m = t(n); must pick r large enough so that p(n) ≤ t(n).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - Forgetting that writing/querying a string of length t(n) costs O(t(n)) time; ensure t(n) is polynomial so the overall decider remains poly-time.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Completeness:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - Defining S but not describing M with the oracle and not proving equivalence on inputs.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - For P_bad-angel, proving only one direction of inclusion.
