[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Core Principle Identification: We need to count probabilities of color sequences under sampling without replacement. The key facts are: (1) “Happy” means the 5-card row has at most one color change, i.e., a contiguous block of R’s and a contiguous block of G’s (including the degenerate case of all one color). (2) For a specific ordered color sequence with r reds and 5−r greens, its probability is P_single(r) = (5P_r)(5P_{5−r})/(10P_5), where nP_k is the falling factorial n!/(n−k)!. This is because each R position can be filled by distinct reds in 5P_r ways, similarly 5P_{5−r} greens, and all 5-card ordered draws from 10 distinct cards are equally likely (10P_5 total).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Pitfall Prediction:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Conceptual: Misinterpreting “adjacent” and forgetting that all-one-color (RRRRR or GGGGG) is allowed and satisfies the condition (the empty color is trivially contiguous).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Calculation: Treating all color patterns as equally likely without accounting that probabilities differ by r (though within a fixed r, sequences are equally likely).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Completeness: Counting only the number of patterns but not weighting by P_single(r), or forgetting the two orientations for 1 ≤ r ≤ 4.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Strategy Formulation:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 1) Compute the total ordered sample size 10P5 = 10·9·8·7·6.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 2) Derive P_single(r) = (5P_r)(5P_{5−r})/(10P_5). Simplify using 5P_r = 5!/(5−r)! to get P_single(r) = 5!^2/[(5−r)! r! · 10P_5].
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 3) Enumerate multiplicities f(r) of “happy” ordered patterns: f(0)=1, f(5)=1, and for r=1..4, f(r)=2 (R^r G^{5−r} or G^{5−r} R^r).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 4) Compute P_single(r) numerically for r=0..5. Note 5!^2/(10P_5) = 14400/30240 = 10/21, so P_single(r) = (10/21)/[(5−r)! r!].
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 5) Sum P_happy = Σ_{r=0}^5 f(r)·P_single(r), reduce to m/n, then report m+n.
