[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Core Principle Identification:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Impartial combinatorial games (normal play): Positions are winning (N) or losing (P). A position is losing (P) if and only if no allowed move leads to a P-position; it is winning (N) if and only if at least one allowed move leads to a P-position.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - For the subtract-a-square game, moves are n → n − k^2 where k ≥ 1 and k^2 ≤ n. Hence: n ∈ W iff ∃ k with k^2 ≤ n such that n − k^2 ∈ L; otherwise n ∈ L. Base case: 0 ∈ L.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Counting argument over initial positions up to N: Let L(N) be the number of losing positions in {0,1,...,N}, W(N) the number of winning positions. Then L(N) + W(N) = N + 1. Each losing l ≤ N can generate at most floor(√(N − l)) ≤ √N winning positions ≤ N of the form l + k^2. Hence W(N) ≤ Σ_{l ∈ L, l ≤ N} floor(√(N − l)) ≤ L(N)√N. Combining yields L(N) ≥ (N + 1)/(√N + 1), which grows unbounded with N, implying infinitely many losing positions.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Pitfall Prediction:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Conceptual: Mixing up definitions of winning/losing positions; forgetting 0 is losing; assuming equality W(N) = L(N)√N rather than the correct inequality ≤.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Calculation: Failing to restrict to positions ≤ N when counting; using √(N) instead of √(N − l) without justifying the inequality; mishandling floor function (only an upper bound is needed).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Completeness: Not translating “infinitely many losing positions” into the required conclusion that there are infinitely many initial heap sizes where the second player has a forced win.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Strategy Formulation:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 1) Define L (losing) and W (winning) positions with the correct recursive characterization; set 0 ∈ L.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 2) For each N ≥ 0, define L(N), W(N) as counts up to N and note L(N) + W(N) = N + 1.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 3) Show every winning position n ≤ N has a representation n = l + k^2 with l ∈ L, hence W(N) ≤ Σ_{l ≤ N, l ∈ L} number of k with k^2 ≤ N − l = Σ floor(√(N − l)) ≤ L(N)√N.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 4) Combine with step 2 to get L(N) ≥ (N + 1)/(√N + 1).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 5) Conclude L(N) → ∞ as N → ∞. Therefore there are infinitely many losing positions (P-positions). Since starting in a P-position makes the first player lose (and thus the second player has a winning strategy), there are infinitely many initial situations where the second player can force a win.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] </think>
