[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 开始处理问题： Let  $n\geq1$  be a positive integer.  $n$  lamps are placed in a line. At minute 0, some lamps are on (maybe all of them). Every minute the state of the lamps changes: A lamp is on at minute  $t+1$  if and only if at minute  $t$ , exactly one of its neighbors is on (the two lamps at the ends have one neighbor each, all other lamps have two neighbors).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] For which values of  $n$  can we guarantee that all lamps will be off after some time?
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 正在获取解决方案计划...
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] <think>
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Core Principle Identification:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Linearization over GF(2): Encode lamp states at time t as a vector x_t ∈ F2^n (1=on, 0=off). The update rule is linear: x_{t+1} = A x_t over F2, where A is the n×n adjacency matrix of the path graph P_n (ones on the super- and sub-diagonals; endpoints have a single 1 neighbor).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Guarantee ⇔ Nilpotency: “For every initial state, eventually all lamps are off” ⇔ ∃k such that A^k = 0 (A nilpotent). This follows from linearity: x_k = A^k x_0 must be 0 for all x_0.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Characteristic polynomial recurrence in char 2: Let p_n(λ) = det(λI − A_n). For the path adjacency matrix, p_0=1, p_1=λ, and p_n = λ p_{n-1} − p_{n-2}. Over F2, − = +, so p_n(λ) = λ p_{n-1}(λ) + p_{n-2}(λ).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Squaring identities in char 2 (derived from the recurrence): p_{2k}(λ) = p_k(λ)^2 + p_{k−1}(λ)^2 and p_{2k+1}(λ) = λ · p_k(λ)^2. These allow binary-doubling decompositions.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Nilpotency criterion via characteristic polynomial: Over any field, A is nilpotent iff its characteristic polynomial is λ^n (equivalently, 0 is the only eigenvalue and algebraic multiplicity n). In F2, showing p_n(λ) = λ^n gives A^n = 0 by Cayley–Hamilton.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Pitfall Prediction:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Conceptual: Treating endpoints as having two neighbors with a ghost 0 (leading to the infinite-line rule-90 convolution) is incorrect here; endpoints use a one-neighbor rule. Also, “eventually zero for all initial states” is stronger than “some initial states go to zero”; you must use nilpotency, not just existence of absorbing states.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Calculation: Mixing signs in the characteristic polynomial recurrence; in characteristic 2, − becomes +. Forgetting to square polynomials correctly in char 2 (cross terms vanish).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Completeness: Proving sufficiency (n works) but not necessity (no other n works), or failing to link p_n(λ)=λ^n to A^n=0 via Cayley–Hamilton.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Strategy Formulation:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 1) Model the system as x_{t+1} = A x_t over F2 with A the path adjacency matrix.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 2) Argue that “guarantee” ⇔ A nilpotent ⇔ ∃k with A^k=0.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 3) Work with the characteristic polynomial p_n(λ): establish the recurrence p_0=1, p_1=λ, p_n=λ p_{n−1}+p_{n−2} in F2.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 4) Prove identities p_{2k}=p_k^2+p_{k−1}^2 and p_{2k+1}=λ p_k^2 in F2 by induction on k using the recurrence.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 5) Sufficiency: If n=2^m−1, iterate the odd-index identity to get p_n(λ)=λ^{2^m−1}; then by Cayley–Hamilton, A^n=0, so all states die out in ≤ n steps.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 6) Necessity: If n is even, p_n(0)=p_{n−2}(0)=…=p_0(0)=1 ⇒ p_n(λ) has nonzero constant term ⇒ not λ^n. If n is odd but not 2^m−1, write n=2k+1 with k≠2^r−1; then p_n=λ p_k^2 where p_k is not a pure monomial, so p_n has lower-degree terms ⇒ not λ^n ⇒ A not nilpotent.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 7) Conclude: The desired n are exactly n=2^m−1 (m≥1). Optionally state the bound T≤n by A^n=0.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] </think>
