[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 开始处理问题： For her zeroth project at Magic School, Emilia needs to grow six perfectly-shaped apple trees. First she plants six tree saplings at the end of Day  $0$ . On each day afterwards, Emilia attempts to use her magic to turn each sapling into a perfectly-shaped apple tree, and for each sapling she succeeds in turning it into a perfectly-shaped apple tree that day with a probability of  $\frac{1}{2}$ . (Once a sapling is turned into a perfectly-shaped apple tree, it will stay a perfectly-shaped apple tree.) The expected number of days it will take Emilia to obtain six perfectly-shaped apple trees is  $\frac{m}{n}$  for relatively prime positive integers  $m$  and  $n$ . Find  $100m+n$ .
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] *Proposed by Yannick Yao*
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 正在获取解决方案计划...
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] <think>
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Core Principle Identification: We model the day each sapling becomes a perfect tree as an independent geometric random variable Ti with success probability p = 1/2 and support {1,2,3,...}. The day all trees are perfect is M = max(T1,...,T6). Use the tail-sum formula for integer-valued nonnegative (shifted) variables: E[M] = sum_{t=1}^∞ P(M ≥ t). Compute P(M ≥ t) via independence using P(Ti ≤ t-1) = 1 - (1/2)^{t-1}, giving P(M ≥ t) = 1 - [1 - 2^{-(t-1)}]^6. Expand via the binomial theorem and evaluate the resulting geometric series: E[M] = sum_{j=1}^6 C(6,j)(-1)^{j+1} 2^j/(2^j - 1). Then compute the exact rational value and finally 100m + n.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Pitfall Prediction:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Conceptual: Off-by-one in day indexing (attempts start on day 1), or modeling the process as a single negative binomial (it is the maximum of independent geometricals, not the sum).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Calculation: Using P(M > t) vs P(M ≥ t) inconsistently, or using P(Ti ≤ t) with the wrong geometric support; forgetting independence across trees; algebraic errors when expanding and summing the series.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Completeness: Stopping after setting up E[M] as an infinite series without evaluating it to a closed-form rational; not reducing the fraction before computing 100m + n.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Strategy Formulation:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 1. Define Ti ~ Geom(1/2) on {1,2,...} and M = max(T1,...,T6).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 2. Use the tail-sum formula E[M] = sum_{t=1}^∞ P(M ≥ t). Compute P(M ≥ t) by independence: P(M ≥ t) = 1 - [P(Ti ≤ t-1)]^6 with P(Ti ≤ t-1) = 1 - 2^{-(t-1)}, so P(M ≥ t) = 1 - [1 - 2^{-(t-1)}]^6.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 3. Expand 1 - [1 - 2^{-(t-1)}]^6 via the binomial theorem: sum_{j=1}^6 C(6,j)(-1)^{j+1} 2^{-j(t-1)}. Swap sums and evaluate sum_{t=1}^∞ 2^{-j(t-1)} = 1/(1 - 2^{-j}) = 2^j/(2^j - 1).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 4. Compute E[M] = sum_{j=1}^6 C(6,j)(-1)^{j+1} 2^j/(2^j - 1) exactly: 12 - 20 + 160/7 - 16 + 192/31 - 64/63 = 7880/1953; reduce the fraction (gcd = 1) to identify m and n.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 5. Compute 100m + n = 100*7880 + 1953 = 789953.
