[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Core Principle Identification:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Use the identity for the sum of cubes: 1^3 + 2^3 + ... + n^3 = (n(n+1)/2)^2.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Set m = n + 5 and work modulo m, noting n ≡ -5 (mod m) and n+1 ≡ -4 (mod m).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Compute H = n(n+1)/2 modulo m by halving the even factor before reducing: H ≡ (-4/2)·(-5) ≡ 10 (mod m), so S ≡ H^2 ≡ 100 (mod m).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Solve S ≡ 17 (mod m) together with S ≡ 100 (mod m) to get m | (100 − 17) = 83.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Pitfall Prediction:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Conceptual: Illegally “dividing by 2” modulo m when m is even; must halve the even factor first as integers before reducing.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Calculation: Forgetting that n = m − 5 implies the even of {n, n+1} is −4 mod m, not −5.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Completeness: Not enforcing n positive, i.e., m = n + 5 ≥ 6, so excluding m = 1.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Strategy Formulation:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 1. Let S = (n(n+1)/2)^2 and m = n + 5. We need S ≡ 17 (mod m).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 2. Compute H = n(n+1)/2 modulo m by halving the even factor first: with n ≡ -5 and n+1 ≡ -4 (mod m), the even factor is −4, so H ≡ (−4/2)·(−5) = 10 (mod m).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 3. Then S ≡ H^2 ≡ 100 (mod m). Combine with S ≡ 17 (mod m): hence 100 ≡ 17 (mod m) ⇒ m | 83.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 4. Since n is positive, m = n + 5 ≥ 6. The only divisor of 83 ≥ 6 is 83, giving n = 78. Sum the solutions (single value): 78.
