[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Core Principle Identification:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Factor modulus: 2021 = 43 × 47 (both primes).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Multiplicativity and prime-power formula for divisor sums: For a = ∏ p_i^{e_i}, σ(a^n) = ∏ σ(p_i^{n e_i}) with σ(p^t) = 1 + p + … + p^t = (p^{t+1} − 1)/(p − 1).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Modular cases for a prime modulus q:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - If p ≡ 0 (mod q), then σ(p^t) ≡ 1 (mod q).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - If p ≡ 1 (mod q), then σ(p^t) ≡ t + 1 (mod q).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   - If p ≠ 0,1 (mod q), then σ(p^t) ≡ 1 (mod q) ⇔ (p^{t+1} − 1) ≡ (p − 1) (mod q) ⇔ p^t ≡ 1 (mod q).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Group-theoretic exponent: For prime q, the multiplicative group (Z/qZ)^× is cyclic of order q − 1; requiring x^n ≡ 1 for all x ∈ (Z/qZ)^× forces (q − 1) | n.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Chinese Remainder Theorem: Congruences mod 43 and mod 47 combine to mod 2021.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]   
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Pitfall Prediction:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Forgetting the special case p ≡ 1 (mod q), which forces q | n (since σ(p^{ne}) ≡ ne + 1 (mod q) must equal 1 for all e).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Ignoring p ≡ 0 (mod q), though it imposes no constraint (σ ≡ 1 automatically).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Assuming only (q − 1) | n is enough; in fact, both q | n and (q − 1) | n are needed.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Misapplying σ(p^t) formula or mixing t with n and e (t = n e).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Miscomputing lcm(42, 46, 43, 47) or the final sum of distinct prime factors.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] - Misinterpreting “sum of the prime factors” as counting multiplicity; standard reading is sum of distinct prime divisors.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] Strategy Formulation:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 1. Factor 2021 into 43 × 47 and handle congruences separately mod q ∈ {43, 47}.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 2. Reduce to prime powers: since σ is multiplicative, the condition must hold for a = p^e for every prime p and e ≥ 1. Thus require σ(p^{n e}) ≡ 1 (mod q) for all primes p and integers e ≥ 1.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 3. For each prime q:
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]    - Case p ≡ 1 (mod q): σ(p^{n e}) ≡ n e + 1 (mod q) ⇒ to hold for all e, q | n.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]    - Case p ≠ 0,1 (mod q): From σ(p^{n e}) ≡ 1 and σ(p^t) = (p^{t+1} − 1)/(p − 1), deduce p^{n e} ≡ 1 (mod q) for all e; taking e = 1 forces p^n ≡ 1 for all p ∈ (Z/qZ)^×. Hence (q − 1) | n.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98]    - Case p ≡ 0 (mod q): No restriction (σ ≡ 1).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 4. Combine: For q = 43, need 43 | n and 42 | n; for q = 47, need 47 | n and 46 | n. Therefore n must be a multiple of L = lcm(42, 46, 43, 47).
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 5. Compute L via prime factorization: 42 = 2·3·7, 46 = 2·23, 43 and 47 are primes ⇒ L has prime divisors {2, 3, 7, 23, 43, 47}. Minimal n = 2·3·7·23·43·47.
[INFO] 09-22 15:07:01 run_experiments.py:98] 6. The requested output is the sum of the prime factors of n (distinct): 2 + 3 + 7 + 23 + 43 + 47 = 125.
